{"cti_reasoning":"With armed=1, bin=1 and gray=2, the property holds because neither register is zero. The relation is violated because gray(1) is 1, not 2. With start, stop and step all low the design idles here forever. One step adds 1 to bin, giving 2, and XORs gray with the flip mask 2 (the Gray code of bin^(bin+1)=3). That makes gray zero while bin is non-zero, so the property fails.","cti_state":[{"signal":"armed","value":"1'b1"},{"signal":"bin","value":"64'd1"},{"signal":"gray","value":"64'd2"}],"invariants":["gray == (bin ^ (bin >> 1))"],"verilog":"module gray_tally (
    input  wire        clk,
    input  wire        start,
    input  wire        stop,
    input  wire        step,
    output reg  [63:0] bin,
    output reg  [63:0] gray,
    output reg         armed
);
    initial bin   = 64'd0;
    initial gray  = 64'd0;
    initial armed = 1'b0;

    // next binary count and the bits that change on increment
    wire [63:0] bin_nx = bin + 64'd1;
    wire [63:0] diff   = bin ^ bin_nx;
    // binary-to-Gray conversion of the change mask: XOR each bit with its upper neighbour
    wire [63:0] flip   = diff ^ (diff >> 1);

    // tally counters: binary count plus an incrementally maintained Gray image
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (start) begin
            bin  <= 64'd0;
            gray <= 64'd0;
        end else if (step) begin
            bin  <= bin_nx;
            gray <= gray ^ flip;
        end
    end

    // observation window: opened by start, closed by stop (stop may never come)
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (start)
            armed <= 1'b1;
        else if (stop)
            armed <= 1'b0;
    end

    // inside the window the Gray image is zero exactly when the binary count is zero
    assert property (!armed || ((gray == 64'd0) == (bin == 64'd0)));
endmodule","top_module":"gray_tally","clock":"clk","antecedents":["armed"],"sanity_covers":["armed && bin == 64'd3","armed && gray == 64'd2"]}

{"cti_reasoning":"With cnt=1000 and rem=5, P holds because cnt <= 1000, but R fails because 1000+5 != 1000. Holding en low keeps this state for any number of cycles. A single step with en=1, tick=1, load=0 then increments cnt to 1001, since the step is gated only by rem != 0, and that breaks P.","cti_state":[{"signal":"cnt","value":"32'd1000"},{"signal":"rem","value":"32'd5"}],"invariants":["({1'b0, cnt} + {1'b0, rem}) == 33'd1000"],"verilog":"module tta_loop_fu (
